<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  ===================================================================================
	  THIS FILE IS A PARTIAL Info.plist AND IS MERGED WITH THE GENERATED ONE.
	  ===================================================================================

	  GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays YES, so every INFOPLIST_KEY_* build setting still
	  applies; this file adds only the two things that cannot be expressed as a build
	  setting, because both are dictionaries.

	  IT LIVES IN Config/ AND NOT IN Dudo/ ON PURPOSE. The Dudo/ group is a
	  PBXFileSystemSynchronizedRootGroup, so every file under it is added to the target
	  automatically — an Info.plist there would be copied into the bundle as a RESOURCE
	  as well as being processed as the Info.plist, and the build fails with "multiple
	  commands produce Info.plist". Keeping it outside the synchronized folder avoids
	  needing a membership exception to undo an inclusion nobody asked for.
	-->

	<!--
	  The API origin, from the DUDO_API_BASE_URL build setting. Debug points at a local
	  `wrangler dev`; Release is deliberately EMPTY, because no Dudo server is deployed
	  and a Release build carrying a developer's laptop address is a build that fails
	  silently in a tester's hands. An empty value is a state the app understands: the
	  sign-in screen says there is no server and offers the sample data instead.
	-->
	<key>DudoAPIBaseURL</key>
	<string>$(DUDO_API_BASE_URL)</string>

	<!--
	  ===================================================================================
	  APP TRANSPORT SECURITY — ONE NARROW EXCEPTION, AND NOT THE BROAD ONE
	  ===================================================================================

	  NSAllowsLocalNetworking permits cleartext HTTP to loopback, .local names and the
	  private IP ranges, and NOTHING ELSE. Every public host still requires HTTPS with
	  ATS's full requirements intact.

	  It is here because a Dudo backend under development is `wrangler dev` over plain
	  HTTP, reached either as localhost from a simulator or as a Mac's LAN address from a
	  device on the same network. The alternatives were worse: NSAllowsArbitraryLoads
	  would disable ATS for every host including production, and shipping no exception at
	  all would mean the transport could not be exercised on a real device before it is
	  exercised in front of a customer.

	  WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, STATED SO IT IS NOT ASSUMED: it does not make a plain-HTTP
	  connection safe. The derived login secret and the session credential both cross it,
	  and on a shared network both are readable. The sign-in screen shows an explicit
	  "unencrypted connection" warning whenever the configured base URL is not HTTPS, and
	  that warning is the control here — this key only stops the OS from making the
	  development case impossible.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
